2186155985 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186155988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155985 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186155989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155985 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186155990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155985 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155985 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155985 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dc264f7f-ffc3-5bf3-a557-71a3989333ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155986 #edb/ref 1080 "x + 4 =-2t^{2}, y - 2 =-t" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155986 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155986 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8adb0c2d-9f2e-5023-a4de-11d4841456e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155987 #edb/ref 1080 "x - 2 =-4t^{2}, y + 4 =-2t" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155987 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155987 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9c177274-b65f-5b52-961a-76fbd5c0a6d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155988 #edb/ref 1080 "x + 2 =-4t, y - 4 =-2t^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155988 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155988 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d0c77571-ad2d-5d9c-b00f-60d3528e0c61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155989 #edb/ref 1080 "x + 4 = 2t, y - 2 =-t^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155989 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155989 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "178a1de6-939c-5dbc-a02f-0d2a31cca675" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155990 #edb/ref 1080 "x + 2 = 4t, y - 4 = 2t^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155990 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186155990 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1dad44b2-d9b8-506b-b9ad-30e2d70debcb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157107 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7446" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157107 #edb/ref 1071 "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty),$ generates the parabola shown below?\n\n![](images/1a/1a75a5242f3636efac8def7fc62458e1c1c6972ca79a1ab6fd73af47be622a7a.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157107 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1abed37b-99f8-5298-bc7b-f80259c78398" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157107 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(-4,5),$ and passes through the point $(-2,3).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx - h = 2pt, \\qquad y - k = pt^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k) = (-4,5),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx + 4 = 2pt, \\qquad y - 5 = pt^2.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-2, 3),$ we can substitute $x=-2$ and $y=3$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=2pt \\\\ -2=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx + 4 = 2\\left(-\\dfrac12\\right)t, \\qquad y - 5 = \\left(-\\dfrac12\\right)t^2 ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx + 4 = -t, \\qquad y - 5 = -\\dfrac12t^2 .\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157108 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7447" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157108 #edb/ref 1071 "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty),$ generates the parabola shown below?\n\n![](images/83/8355891fbc605593a32dd100ee8c1c2e4324da0630755984158ec23f6d419ab9.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157108 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "af2757f8-0392-54c8-b681-162ee13b1804" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157108 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(-3,2),$ and passes through the point $(1,0).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx - h = pt^2, \\qquad y - k = 2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k) = (-3,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+3 = pt^2, \\qquad y-2 = 2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(1,0),$ we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=0$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=p{t}^{2} \\\\ -2=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =p{(-\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 4 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+3 = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)t^2, \\qquad y -2 = 2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)t ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+3 = \\dfrac{1}{4}t^2, \\qquad y -2= \\dfrac{1}{2}t .\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157109 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7448" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157109 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-1 = -2t^2, \\qquad y+1 = -4t, \\qquad t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157109 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "780170af-a92e-5a87-b08d-d9d940d6906b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157109 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-\\frac{x-1}{2},\\,t=-\\frac{y+1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{y+1}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation $t^2 = -\\dfrac{x-1}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16} & =-\\frac{x-1}{2} \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =16\\cdot (-\\frac{x-1}{2}) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =-8(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157303 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157303 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157303 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157303 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157303 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157303 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157303 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157303 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157303 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157303 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157303 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157303 #edb/ref 1039 "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157303 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157303 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "61e9c047-0bbf-560d-b803-87703164db5b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157304 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157304 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157304 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157304 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157304 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157304 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157304 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157304 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157304 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157304 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157304 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157304 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157304 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157304 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157304 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157304 #edb/ref 1039 "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157304 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157304 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157305 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157846 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157305 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157305 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157305 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157305 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157305 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186243894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157305 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157305 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157305 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157305 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157305 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157305 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157305 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157305 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157305 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157305 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157305 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157305 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157305 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157305 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157305 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157305 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157492 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157492 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157492 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157492 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157492 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157492 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157492 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157492 #edb/ref 1045 "Parametric Equations of Parabolas Centered at (h,k)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157492 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186158180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157492 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157492 #edb/ref 1050 1020.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186157492 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "9c101306-1057-58f9-ac1a-03ead5aea493" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157842 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186155973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157842 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84459" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157842 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$, generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(1,-2)$ and which passes through the point $(-3, 6)$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157842 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e847f0a2-135f-5fe9-88a5-7da062fb29a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157843 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157843 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84462" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157843 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157843 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y-1)^{2}=-5(x-1)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157843 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b9821351-2cd3-5e5a-a4b5-1b6135a99522" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157843 #edb/ref 1074 "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(1,1).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx-1=p{t}^{2},\\,y-1=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =-5(x-1) \\\\ 4p(x-1) & =-5(x-1) \\\\ 4p & =-5 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-1=(-\\frac{5}{4}){t}^{2},\\,y-1=2(-\\frac{5}{4})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-1=-\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-\\frac{5}{2}t.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157844 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186155985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157844 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84464" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157844 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/34/34e9beebbecc3eff77faa9b01f4684e334ec7cbdcbd9b00e328f95f7be88c1d4.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$, generate the parabola shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157844 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6266b55e-c4d6-52a5-b8a7-d0b0754b47be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157845 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157845 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84467" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157845 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157845 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/75/75df54dd30da590814e89153396ac7cf8a713fc9a6e04d14786780147ba57cfe.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157845 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "367eead1-51e7-5f05-9cdc-3a8e5cc1c6dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157845 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(5,-1),$ and passes through the point $(17,11).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(5,-1),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-5=p{t}^{2},\\,y+1=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(17,11),$ we can substitute $x=17$ and $y=11$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}12=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 12=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =2pt \\\\ 6 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{6}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =p{(\\frac{6}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =p\\cdot \\frac{36}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{36}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{36}{12} \\\\ p & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-5=(3){t}^{2},\\,y+1=2(3)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-5=3{t}^{2},\\,y+1=6t.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157846 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157846 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84469" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157846 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157846 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-6=3t,\\,y-3=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157846 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8b1571ae-cba4-599c-b064-df002d19a987" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157846 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{x-6}{3},\\,{t}^{2}=\\frac{2(y-3)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{x-6}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{2(y-3)}{3}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{9} & =\\frac{2(y-3)}{3} \\\\ (x-6)^{2} & =9\\cdot (\\frac{2(y-3)}{3}) \\\\ (x-6)^{2} & =6(y-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158148 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158148 #edb/ref 1061 27633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158148 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "99f10468-a948-583a-88a5-6e0b1090ba79" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158148 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158149 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158149 #edb/ref 1061 27634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158149 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3d1e229c-5dac-5677-a852-b2257436bd7c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158149 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158150 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158150 #edb/ref 1061 27635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158150 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c1826ce1-bb63-5660-9720-c1e180003184" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158150 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158180 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158180 #edb/ref 1061 28496 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158180 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bcfe026f-8c08-5f21-a2e2-5d9aa2ef5726" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158180 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158181 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158181 #edb/ref 1061 28498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158181 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e3279e8f-1b3f-59cf-b3a2-dedf1b46f31b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158181 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158182 #edb/ref 1061 28500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158182 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6ab7a388-97c9-5750-8334-4e93498973b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158182 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158498 #edb/ref 1064 "The parametric equations of a *vertical* parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h = 2pt, \\qquad y-k = pt^2, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nFor instance, the parabola \n\n$$\nx-2 = 2t,\\quad y-1 = t^2,\\quad t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)\n$$\n\nhas its vertex at $(h,k) = (2,1)$ and has $p = 1.$ A sketch of this parabola is shown below.\n\n![](images/03/036d1dff3c5daada329489b55259369e1dece17be7b1af7ff0314eb47318a9ce.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158498 #edb/ref 1065 4627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158498 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e03fed66-1fc4-5d82-a378-4f47605f8e17" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158498 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158499 #edb/ref 1064 "The parametric equations of a *horizontal* parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h = pt^2, \\qquad y-k = 2pt, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158499 #edb/ref 1065 4628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158499 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "badab523-af9e-55ff-b5e0-cd66ae697426" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158499 #edb/ref 1067 "Parametric Equations of Horizontal Parabolas" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158500 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's recall the parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k){:}$\n\n$$\nx-h = 2pt, \\qquad y-k = pt^2, \\qquad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nHow can we be sure that these equations describe a parabola?\n\nFirst, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-h & =2pt & ⟹ &  & t & =\\frac{x-h}{2p} \\\\ y-k & =p{t}^{2} & ⟹ &  & {t}^{2} & =\\frac{y-k}{p}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring the first equation above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{x-h}{2p})}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{4{p}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting in the second equation $t^2 = \\dfrac{y-k}{p},$ we can eliminate the parameter $t$ and rearrange to find the Cartesian equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{4{p}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{y-k}{p} & =\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{4{p}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{y-k}{p}\\cdot 4{p}^{2} & =(x-h)^{2} \\\\ (x-h)^{2} & =4p(y-k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the Cartesian equation of a vertical parabola with vertex $(h,k).$ Therefore, the parametric equations do indeed describe a vertical parabola.\n\nIf we did the same for the parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k),$ we would obtain the Cartesian equation of a horizontal parabola, namely $(y-k)^2 = 4p(x-h).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158500 #edb/ref 1065 4629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158500 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "aa16807b-02e7-5313-af63-c2996410568b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158500 #edb/ref 1067 "Justification That the Parametric Equations Describe a Parabola" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186299939 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186299939 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186299939 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "01f966e1-32ba-571c-8d2f-139d236a9efb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300393 #edb/ref 1080 "25(y-2)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300393 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300393 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "53b2515b-c470-5df9-b5f0-72da0d8839dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301454 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186300393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301454 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301454 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301454 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186300393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301454 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4339e742-90db-5fe5-8713-242c87bd162a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301901 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186299939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301901 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301901 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301901 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186299939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301901 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d17ed819-e5df-5f2a-85be-61fbd0c56120" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302072 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302072 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186301901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302072 #edb/ref 1069 "q-321592" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302072 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302072 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola \n\n$$\nx+2=5t,\\,y-2={t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302072 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "df968d14-24c9-53a0-ac49-2683ed9a8cca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302072 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{x+2}{5},\\,{t}^{2}=y-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{x+2}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=y-2$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{25} & =y-2 \\\\ {(x+2)}^{2} & =25(y-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351563 #edb/ref 1080 "x+5=-{t}^{2},\\,y+3=6t" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351563 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351563 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "003023ae-1aa2-54a4-98df-d8e7ff22066d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351612 #edb/ref 1080 "x+5=-4{t}^{2},\\,y+3=12t" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351612 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351612 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "04a61724-2b44-5a05-b9c5-ee7e18a029b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351888 #edb/ref 1080 "(x-6)^{2}=24(y-3)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351888 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351888 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "23911304-8fc2-5561-9525-01cac991662b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351902 #edb/ref 1080 "x-5=-3{t}^{2},\\,y-3=3t" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351902 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351902 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "249bff29-1e23-57f0-a40d-6219e1c53f7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351906 #edb/ref 1080 "x-5=-3{t}^{2},\\,y-3=-6t" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351906 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351906 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "252094cb-c77d-5625-a2e6-f8d7aa811973" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351986 #edb/ref 1080 "x+1=-3t,\\,y+2=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351986 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351986 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2d949e01-5c39-571d-88f1-dcb4d3a1a2ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352006 #edb/ref 1080 "x-1=-3{t}^{2},\\,y+5=-6t" #edb/