id "ef32ef9c-fd4b-5387-a414-4da52d467787" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354229 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186352006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354229 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186352340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354229 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186352715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354229 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186353143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354229 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186353177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354229 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354229 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354229 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186353143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354229 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ef4358c3-f15f-501b-ba43-cb878c186919" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354344 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354344 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84819" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354344 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354344 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d9/d963d5620df91a05c8371ef09beeb84546c5f5a8cda047f2c2c3b9933f3d5c01.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354344 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7c9ce835-d453-5565-8c95-6082447bc008" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354344 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(2,-2),$ and passes through the point $(4,2).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(2,-2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-2=2pt,\\,y+2=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,2),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=2$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=2pt \\\\ 4=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 4 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-2=2(\\frac{1}{4})t,\\,y+2=(\\frac{1}{4}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-2=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354345 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354345 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84821" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354345 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354345 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/91/91500faad34245d5b2389ae71aa39f34b10b9a75b1b75307b5aa6231b311307b.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354345 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0f9a3da7-8717-5e67-b44f-7ce603eaf516" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354345 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(1,2),$ and passes through the point $(7,-4).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(1,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-1=2pt,\\,y-2=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(7,-4),$ we can substitute $x=7$ and $y=-4$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6=2pt \\\\ -6=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =2pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{3}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6 & =p{(\\frac{3}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -6 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -6 & =\\frac{9}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-1=2(-\\frac{3}{2})t,\\,y-2=(-\\frac{3}{2}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-1=-3t,\\,y-2=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354353 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354353 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84844" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354353 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354353 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y-3)^{2}=-12(x-5)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354353 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1303c715-2341-5c20-852f-40641cdc1a09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354353 #edb/ref 1074 "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(5,3).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx-5=p{t}^{2},\\,y-3=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =-12(x-5) \\\\ 4p(x-5) & =-12(x-5) \\\\ 4p & =-12 \\\\ p & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-5=(-3){t}^{2},\\,y-3=2(-3)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-5=-3{t}^{2},\\,y-3=-6t.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428775 #edb/ref 1030 0.5 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428775 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity n